4. what this does to the numbers that were flagged provisional
================================================================================================================
  the ~5 sigma external-field-SLOPE negative (cluster-infall BTFR, Local Volume dwarfs) was computed with the naive
  nu(x_e) coupling.  A slope is d/dln(x_e) of the log of the coupling, so what matters is not the coupling's VALUE but
  how its LOGARITHM runs with x_e.  Compute both:
        x_e   dln[nu]/dln x   dln[nu(1+L/3)]/dln x   dln[nu(1+L)]/dln x
     0.0010         -0.4921                -0.4906              -0.4844
     0.0100         -0.4754                -0.4706              -0.4524
     0.1000         -0.4251                -0.4114              -0.3636
     0.3000         -0.3755                -0.3541              -0.2857
     1.0000         -0.2910                -0.2597              -0.1716
  [PASS] A4 (AGAINST THE FRAMEWORK'S INTEREST) the sphere-averaged correction barely changes the predicted SLOPE, which is the quantity the ~5 sigma negative measures: dln(coupling)/dln(x_e) moves by under 25% across the whole range, so the negative does NOT dissolve into it   (worst fractional change in the predicted slope = 10.7%; e.g. at x_e = 0.1 the naive slope is -0.4251 and the sphere-averaged one -0.4114)

================================================================================================================
VERDICT
================================================================================================================
  The flux theorem is right and the factor it implies is real, but it was attached to the wrong observable, and this
  script corrects the correction.  nu(1 + L) is the coupling PARALLEL to the external field, and in the deep limit
  that is indeed half of nu.  An isotropic velocity dispersion, and any sphere-averaged enclosed mass, measure the
  ANGLE AVERAGE instead, and the numerical quadrature says that is nu(1 + L/3) = 0.83 nu deep -- a 17% reduction, not
  a factor 2.  So the pressure-supported liabilities shrink by about 0.08 dex, not by 0.3, and the external-field
  SLOPE negative shrinks hardly at all, because a slope depends on how the coupling's logarithm runs and not on its
  value.  REPORTED AGAINST INTEREST: I flagged that ~5 sigma negative as provisional on a factor 2, and by this
  calculation the factor that applies to it is not 2.  The negative stands until the correction run says otherwise
  on the measurement side, and the caveat I attached to the liability table is too generous to the framework.

